  {
    "episode_id": 73277,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/3/73277.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 11,
    "turn_text": "I sure can, you know, inflation basically amounts to attacks on all Americans. It is no discriminator of of economic class of education of sex race or status. It is a tax on everybody because everybody pays to fill up their car with gas. Everybody pays for groceries at the grocery store or retail products, whether it's clothing or things at the hardware store or whatever that is. Right now we have a tremendous inflation problem. We're looking at the highest inflation that we've had in a little over 40 years. And there's two major drivers to that inflation, which is the second part of what I think you want to talk about. And one driver of inflation is energy. You know, just a few short years ago, America was energy independent. It's the first time we were energy independent in nearly in 70 years. Today, we have pulled back because of policies here in Washington from this administration. We've pulled back from the leadership table with our energy policies. And we're about 1.5 million barrels of oil a day off of our peak capacity of 13 million barrels of all a day when we were when we were energy independent. And so what happens when when we have a slowdown of energy production and a restriction on pipelines to take that energy to market is that it depresses energy production. If we're not producing oil and gas, then we don't have that resource to get to market. And Pastor, I don't need to tell you this up until just a couple of weeks ago, we were buying about 500,000 barrels of oil and petroleum products per day from Russia. Here at a time when Russia is conducting this tragic invasion of the nation of Ukraine and America, among many other of our friends and allies, we're paying Russia somewhere between $50 and $75 million a day. for the oiling gas that we took off of the markets with the policies, bad policies here in the United States. So that's what's driving the cost of gasoline up. It's our own lack of production here in the United States. We need to get back to producing from federal lands, from public lands, and we need to expedite permits so that we can get that resource to market. That's what's going to bring down the cost of heating our homes, fueling our cars, transporting groceries to the shelves in grocery stores, and you name it. The other factor that is influencing the skyrocketing inflation is the other topic that you mentioned, spending. When Washington spends money the way that we're spending right now when there is that much cash into the system and over the last two years when you consider what we've spent for the pandemic. And what we've spent by the Biden administration since he came into office with the American Rescue Plan and the infrastructure plan, you name it, we've spent almost six plus trillion dollars of money that we've had to borrow from other countries. When you put that much cash into the system, Pastor, it devalues the dollar. What you're going to spend what you're going to spend a dollar on today is going to cost you $2 or maybe even $4 tomorrow. That's what inflation is. When there are so many dollars out there, people are going to want more of them. for the goods and services that they provide. So two major drivers to inflation, energy costs and the amount of cash that's in the system. We've got to stop spending money that we don't have and we've got to get back to producing our own energy in America.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_02",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
