    ],
    "turn_type_label": "Substantive",
    "conversation_move_label": "Topic Shift"
  },
  {
    "episode_id": 73277,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/3/73277.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 13,
    "turn_text": "You betcha. Well, you know, we didn't get there overnight. And we certainly did not get there during the pandemic. We got there over decades and decades of outsourcing America's labor force to low-cost labor places like Mexico and China and India and other countries. And by doing so, we began to give our intellectual property a way too, because you have to tell those countries how to make the things that you want them to make to then send back to you. And eventually, they learn from that. Over a period of decades, problems with both political parties, we have allowed our intellectual property and our ability to meet our own basic needs for goods and services every day to be outsourced to other countries. What the pandemic did was all of that was shut down. All of that was shut down. We couldn't get products from China. We couldn't get products from Mexico because travel was restricted and workers couldn't work. because countries had put people under masks and mandates that kept them at home. There's only so much you can do to produce products by sitting at home. You got to be able to get to the factory. You got to be able to ship. You got to be able to have your harbors and your docks open to be able to receive those goods and services. So what we learned was that America does not manufacture the very things that we need to sustain our daily lives, things like pharmaceuticals, things like batteries, things like protective equipment for our frontline workers in hospitals, masks and gowns and ventilators and those kinds of things. And we learned a very, very tough lesson. I don't think that there's a person that I serve within Washington DC that doesn't now understand that America can never again outsource more than the basic level of a particular item that we need to meet our own needs. Now, absolutely, we've got to play in this global market. I mean, there are there are billions of customers all over the world. If America is going to maintain itself as the largest economy on the planet, we've got to be able to have access to those markets where we can get to those customers. What does that mean? It means that we've got to be able to trade. We've got to have fair and equitable trade, but we should never outsource those kind of those things more than what we need for our own uses right here in the United States. So that's kind of where we are with the supply chain and we're We're taking some major initiatives to what we're calling re-homing that supply chain back to the United States. Let's start making it back here, and a lot of companies have come back to the United States already.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_02",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
    "explicit_propositions": [
