    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/3/73277.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 15,
    "turn_text": "Absolutely. And Pastor, I agree with that. I agree with that. In fact, if you look at some of the compelling statistics from countries that never shut down, never put their people under mask mandates, never even mandated vaccines for their people. Their numbers with COVID infections and the mortality rate in those countries were not dissimilar from other countries that did shut down. The difference is that their economies didn't suffer because of it. So, you know, it's easy to be on Monday morning quarterback and everybody was fearful when the COVID virus first came out. I mean, you know, in my mind, it conjured up images of the blue bonic plague, you know, that everybody was going to get it. And we were going to have people just falling out and dying in the street. It was like fear of the walking dead or something. We were just going to be piling up on the sidewalks. Well, that did not happen. Did a lot of people die? Yes, they did. Is that unfortunate? Yes, it is because every life is sacred. However, I don't believe and I do believe that you are correct. I don't believe that shutting down our economy had the effect of controlling the virus or dampening the virus that people expected it to. And I dare say if we experience something like that again in the future, you might see very different decisions being made.",
